00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:36.590
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): And then I need to go back… I need more feedback on it, but I could read it out today.

267
00:27:37.180 --> 00:27:52.519
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): is a bit long. I don't know if I can shorten. Imagine Alice applies for a job, and the employer wants to verify her university degree. She presents a digitally signed credential from her university. Just like a handwritten signature, this digital signature proves the university really issued it.

268
00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:59.389
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): And the employer can confirm that by checking the university's public key and its DID document. So far, so good.

269
00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:19.510
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): But digital security best practices says keys should rotate. Universities regularly replace their signing keys to say, stay secure, and their DID document updates accordingly with the latest public key. Depending on the DID method, you might even be able to look up old keys. Now, here's the problem. What happens if an old key gets compromised?

270
00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:31.879
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Suppose someone steals a past signing key. They could generate a fake degree and sign it with that old key. When the employer checks, the signatures will look valid, because it is cryptographically valid for that key.

271
00:28:31.880 --> 00:28:40.290
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): And without more information, the employer cannot tell the difference between a legitimate degree issued years ago and a fraudulent one created yesterday.

272
00:28:40.700 --> 00:28:59.599
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): To try and fix this, you could try publishing when a key is compromised, but you might never learn that it was stolen. Or you could reissue every single past degree with the newest key, but in practice, that quickly becomes unmanageable, especially for large institutions with years of records. This is where anchored signatures come in.

273
00:28:59.850 --> 00:29:13.099
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Instead of relying on the key itself, the university keeps a tamper-evident log of every degree ever issued. Each time they sign a new credential with a given key, they add a record to the tamper-evident list log or ledger.

274
00:29:13.140 --> 00:29:26.649
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): If someone later tries to forge a degree with an old compromised key, the forgery won't appear in the university's historical log. Even though the signature is valid, the credential is missing the critical anchor, proof that it was actually issued.

275
00:29:26.860 --> 00:29:37.510
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): To verify a credential, Alice's employer now checks two things, the signature and the histories. If the degree is on the log, it's legitimate. If not, someone tried to cheat.

276
00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:47.160
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): There are multiple ways to make this history tamper-evident. One approach is anchoring data or cryptographic hashes on a blockchain, where many independent nodes observe and preserve the record.

277
00:29:47.160 --> 00:30:02.099
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Another approach, used by methods like Cary and did WebS, relies on distributed watchers and witnesses that collectively monitor and sign the sequence of events. In both cases, the key idea is the same, a transparent, append-only history that cannot be silently rewritten.

278
00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:15.210
Carly Huitema (University of Guelph): Anchored signatures turn key rotation from a vulnerability into a strength. Even if a passkey is compromised, the integrity of the issued credentials remains intact, because the truth is anchored in the logged history, not just the key.

